<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Zimbabwe</h1><p class="st-meta">1980 CE – present · capital Harare</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A landlocked southern African republic, independent since April 1980 after a 15-year guerrilla war, ruled by ZANU–PF since independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the white minority government declared independence as Rhodesia in 1965, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war followed. In 1979 the country became Zimbabwe Rhodesia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abel-muzorewa">Abel Muzorewa</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancaster-house">Lancaster House</a> Agreement of 21 December 1979 returned it briefly to British rule as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-rhodesia">Southern Rhodesia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-mugabe">Robert Mugabe</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-national-union">ZANU</a> party won the 1980 election, and Zimbabwe became independent on 11 April 1980.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> Banana was the first president, a mainly ceremonial post, while Mugabe was prime minister. Unrest in Matabeleland led to the Gukurahundi campaign by the Fifth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a>, in which estimates of deaths range from 3,750 to 80,000. It ended in 1987 with a unity agreement that created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-national-union">ZANU</a>–PF, and Mugabe became president that year. From 2000 the government pressed ahead with Fast Track Land Reform, compulsory acquisition of white-owned farmland, while the economy declined and hyperinflation marked the later years. In 2008 a power-sharing agreement let Morgan Tsvangirai become prime minister, and a new constitution was approved in 2013.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In November 2017 the army moved against Mugabe after the dismissal of Vice-president Emmerson Mnangagwa, and Mugabe resigned on 21 November 2017. Mnangagwa succeeded him and won the 2018 election, which Nelson Chamisa contested in court. In August 2023 Mnangagwa won a second term in an election rejected by the opposition and questioned by observers.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zimbabwe.jpg" alt="Zimbabwe" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Zimbabwe.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1980 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Harare</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Canaan Banana, Robert Mugabe, Emmerson Mnangagwa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (84% by a 2017 survey), indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shona, Ndebele, English (16 official languages)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Zimbabwe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2003&amp;focus=zimbabwe" aria-label="Zimbabwe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Zimbabwe lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g030s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5086 3967L5084 3970M5086 3967L5001 4066"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5084 3970h0M5001 4066h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5086 3967h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5086 3967h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Harare. <a href="/atlas?year=2003&amp;focus=zimbabwe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
